WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.260
 Japanese food is amazing
in that it's so simple.

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:06.450
It is made up of so
many little dishes

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:11.400
that it feels like little tiny
gifts while you're eating.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.560
When I'm not in
the kitchen, I feel

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:18.960
very self-conscious about how
to express who I am as a person.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:20.850
When I really want
to express something,

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:25.320
it's so easy to do it
through the medium of food.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:29.280
Kaiseki has such a long
history, but recognizing that,

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.890
we've decided to do a
modern version of kaiseki,

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:35.400
because traditional
places in Japanese history

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:37.510
do not necessarily
have space for women.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:45.090
My definition of modern kaiseki
is taking traditional kaiseki

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:47.700
and incorporating
it with new ideas,

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.700
but always remembering
the core philosophy,

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.760
which is honor the ingredient,
be grateful, and do the best

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.880
to showcase those ingredients.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:03.050
I'm Niki Nakayama, and
this is my MasterClass.

00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:04.599
I'm looking forward
to showing you

00:01:04.599 --> 00:01:08.320
how to fabricate some
fish, how to steam a dish,

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:10.390
how to make dashi,
the foundation

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:12.100
of all Japanese cuisine.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:15.370
This is Carole, my wife at
home, and my partner at work,

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:17.890
and we're going to talk about
different Japanese cooking

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:20.680
techniques, and how you
can enjoy it as a family,

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:22.220
or as individual dishes.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:26.230
I'm going to show you this very
simple process of washing rice.

00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:28.810
Letting it sit and
rest for at least 20

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:32.950
to 30 minutes is key to a
really beautiful batch of rice.

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:35.650
We'll be teaching you
how to cut some sashimi,

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:39.220
how to make some tempura, and
how to grill over binchōtan.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:41.080
It looks so beautiful,
so welcoming,

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:42.940
and there's so much life to it.

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:44.620
As you're learning
these techniques,

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:46.600
be kind to yourself,
let yourself

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:49.510
do things that don't always
work, but always learn

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:51.220
from them.

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:53.500
What I hope people who
cook try to communicate

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:56.080
when they make a
meal is a feeling

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:58.240
of love and appreciation.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:00.310
I think it's such an
expression of giving,

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:02.680
and it's such an
expression of care.

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:05.648
That is what I love
most about food.

00:02:05.648 --> 00:02:08.018
Nakayama Niki to moushimasu.

00:02:08.018 --> 00:02:11.548
Kore ga watashi no MasterClass.

00:02:11.548 --> 00:02:15.789
[MUSIC PLAYING]

